WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
SPEAKER 1: Welcome to
Google Cloud Drawing

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.500
Board, where we doodle
our way through the Cloud.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.710
Today's topic-- What is
Google Kubernetes Engine?

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.450
This video is divided
into chapters.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.070
Watch the full
video or skip ahead

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.120
to any section of your choice.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.740
Now, Sam, the system admin,
calls Aaron, the developer.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.300
Her application has crashed.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.030
But it worked just fine
on her workstation.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.520
They check the logs, debug
stuff, and eventually

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.780
version inconsistencies.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.980
The right dependencies were
missing in production--

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.300
no surprise there.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.170
Together, they perform
a risky rollback,

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.700
will later install the
missing dependencies,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.740
and hope nothing else breaks.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.070
Aaron and Sam decide to
fix the root problem once

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.830
and for all using containers.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.360
Containers decouple the OS from
the application dependencies

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.330
in the code.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.780
Due to this abstraction, Sam
can log into each machine

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.420
and instruct it to run
Aaron's containers.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.550
It will pull down
just the files that

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:00.360
have changed since the last
container and run the new code.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.850
If you need to roll
back, all the old files

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.060
are still there, as container
images are immutable.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.580
By using containers,
Aaron and Sam

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.300
solved the "it worked
on my machine" problem.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.450
Containers help improve
portability, credibility,

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.660
deployment speed,
reusabillity, and more.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.280
Turns out Sam's responsible
for more developers than just

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.040
Aaron.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.640
With large number of developers
containerizing their apps,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.330
Sam needs a better way to
orchestrate all the containers

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.310
that these developers
are shipping.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.170
Solution?

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:34.290
Kubernetes.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.980
Kubernetes is a portable,
extensible open source

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.800
platform for managing
containerized workloads

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.170
and services.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.100
But turns out,
Kubernetes is not that

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.830
simple to manage, from
installation, to provisioning,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.120
to upgrades, SLAs, and scaling.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.930
So Sam looks into Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.540
GKE is a managed service
for running Kubernetes.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.540
Apart from making it easy
for you to create clusters,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.720
it offers some advanced
cluster management features,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.990
including load balancing,
auto scaling, auto upgrades,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.030
auto repairs, logging,
monitoring, and more.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.330
Now, how does GKE work?

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.030
All Kubernetes objects
in your containerized app

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.810
run on top of a cluster, which
is the foundation of GKE.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.350
Cluster consists of at least one
control plane and one or more

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.620
machines called nodes, which
are created during the cluster

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.880
creation process.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.390
The control plane includes
the Kubernetes API server,

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.840
scheduler, storage, and
core resource controllers.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.410
The control bay is responsible
for deciding what runs

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.340
on all the cluster's nodes.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.910
This can include scheduling
workloads, managing networks,

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.660
storage, lifecycle,
scaling, and upgrades.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.540
Now, the nodes-- a
node runs the services

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.330
necessary to support the
containers that make up

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.040
your clusters workloads.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.190
These include the container
runtime and the Kubernetes node

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.890
agent, Kublet, which
communicates with the control

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.580
plane and is responsible for
starting and running containers

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.830
as scheduled on that node.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.250
Pods are the smallest, most
basic deployable objects

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.430
in Kubernetes, and contain
one or more Containers.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.490
Pods also contain shared
networking and storage

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.850
resources for their containers.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.892
Now, how do you GKE?

00:03:18.892 --> 00:03:21.660
We know that GKE works
with containerized apps.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.870
So before you deploy a
workload on GKE cluster,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.420
you must first package
it into a container.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.280
To create a
continuous integration

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.080
and continuous
delivery pipeline,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.690
you can use Cloud Code
to write your apps,

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.120
send the code to a
source code repository,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.180
which kicks off a build
process in Cloud Build,

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.190
leading to container
images, which can then

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.710
be stored in Container
Registry, ready to be

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.330
deployed into [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.130
You can then create
the GKE cluster

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.100
using Cloud Console UI,
G Cloud Command Line

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.700
interface, or the API.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.950
Kubectl CLI comes
pre-installed in Cloud Shell

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:59.610
to run commands against
your Kubernetes clusters.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.680
High availability and scaling--

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.860
for availability, you can choose
between two types of clusters,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.360
zonal and regional.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.880
Regional clusters are
better suited for HA

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.040
because they have
multiple control planes

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.190
across multiple
zones in a region,

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:17.100
while zonal clusters have one
control plane in a single zone.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.100
This also means that
changes to cluster config

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.320
takes longer in a
regional cluster

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.010
because they must propagate
across all control planes.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.590
Due to these trade-offs,
choose regional clusters

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.470
when availability is more
important than flexibility,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.070
and use zonal cluster to create
or upgrade clusters rapidly

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.070
when availability is
less of a concern.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.910
GKE also provides four types
of autoscaling for workloads

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:46.170
and infrastructure-- horizontal
pod autoscaler for adding

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.590
and removing pods based on
utilization metrics like CPU

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.380
and memory; vertical
pod autoscaler

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.170
for sizing your pods;
cluster autoscaler

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.270
for adding and
removing nodes based

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.600
on the scheduled workload;
node auto-provisioning

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.000
for dynamically
creating new node

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.810
boards with nodes that match
the needs of your users' pods.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.020
How to secure your
app using GKE--

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.560
GKE is secure by default,
with automatic data encryption

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.420
at rest and in transit.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.600
The OS images you deploy
are Google certified.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.570
You can access your
clusters without public IP

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:22.650
on the internet.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.310
And you can control access using
identity and access management

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.950
and role-based access controls.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:32.430
Additionally, with GKE,
you get trusted networking.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.640
Using global VPC,
you can connect to

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.120
and isolate clusters.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.600
Using global load balancing,
you can deploy public services

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.510
behind a single
global Anycast IP.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:46.160
Using Cloud Armor, you get
easy protection against Layer 7

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.430
and DDoS attacks.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.410
And using networking
policies, you

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:53.380
can control the communication
between your cluster pods.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.090
GKE also comes with tools
to verify, and enforce,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.490
and improve the security
of your infrastructure.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.770
You get binary
authorization to ensure

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:05.470
only properly signed containers
are deployed to production.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.430
Vulnerability scanning
of the container images

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.930
find security vulnerabilities
early on in the CI/CD pipeline.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.270
And since the base
images are managed,

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.880
they are automatically
patched and updated

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.430
for security vulnerabilities.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.460
With all that said, if you are
looking to quickly start with

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.580
containers, check out
GKE at cloud.google.com

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.880
/kubernetesengine.

